WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.460
Some were highly anticipated, some looked
intriguing on the surface but failed to deliver,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.040
and some struggled to even make the public
aware of their existence, but they all have

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.680
one thing in common: they tanked badly at
the box office.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:19.060
Solo: A Star Wars Story opened in May to a
shrug from audiences, and its underwhelming

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:23.640
box office left it the first film in the Star
Wars franchise to be branded a bona fide flop.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.140
The red flags seem obvious in retrospect:
from original directors Phil Lord and Chris

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.320
Miller departing late in production to incoming
director Ron Howard reshooting three-quarters

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.880
of the movie, it seemed as if everything that
could go wrong did.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:41.480
While Donald Glover provided a bright spot
as a young Lando Calrissian, Solo felt unnecessary.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:45.420
Star Wars fatigue may also have been a factor
in the film's failure, and the decision to

00:00:45.429 --> 00:00:49.340
open the film while Avengers: Infinity War
was still in theaters may have played a part

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:53.340
as well - somewhat ironically, it wasn't just
one thing that sunk Solo.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:57.920
Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson starring in your
picture is usually a pretty safe indication

00:00:57.920 --> 00:00:59.120
of box-office success.

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.760
"Daddy's gotta go to work."

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.360
Unfortunately, 2018's Skyscraper left audiences
supremely uninterested - perhaps due to the

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:17.240
fact that the film leaned a little too heavily
into its throwback action movie roots.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:20.660
Even critics who were entertained found it
to be incredibly derivative.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:26.180
With a $125 million budget and an expensive
advertising campaign to match, Skyscraper

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:30.060
would have had to do gangbusters overseas
to avoid losing money, and it didn't quite

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:30.780
manage it.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:35.660
Whether crowds were turned off by the familiar
story or experiencing a bit of a Rock fatigue,

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:38.380
Skyscraper turned out to be a whiff for the
magnetic star.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.740
The John Travolta biopic Gotti got a lot of
press due to its star and subject, infamous

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.680
mob boss John Gotti, leading up to its screening
at Cannes.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.080
But its reception at the festival was less
than rapturous, and upon its June release

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.740
in the U.S., few critics even bothered to
review it.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.180
Literally every single one that did heavily
panned the film.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:01.040
Every aspect of the film's production was
blasted, from its lack of coherent themes

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.200
and ham-fisted direction to Travolta's "distractingly
terrible" lead performance.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:09.640
The movie failed to even make back half of
its paltry $10 million budget, and those involved

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.600
in its production only need to watch the film
to see why Gotti flopped so hard.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.580
Alpha tells the story of a prehistoric youth
who becomes separated from his hunting group

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.080
and partners up with a lone wolf in order
to survive.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.459
It's a thrilling, gorgeously-shot adventure,
but while the film scored with critics, it

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:27.100
failed to connect with audiences.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.980
With an advertising campaign which seemed
to be aimed more squarely at dog lovers than

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:35.090
fans of epic adventure, Alpha simply didn't
generate the kind of positive word of mouth

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:35.680
that it needed.

00:02:36.109 --> 00:02:39.940
It also had the misfortune of squaring off
against Crazy Rich Asians and The Meg, two

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.480
surprising hits that cleaned up at the box
office.

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.900
Alpha failed to make back its budget domestically,
but it seems destined for a robust second

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:47.900
life on home video.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.940
After years of development, Brian Henson - the
son of legendary Muppets creator Jim Henson

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.720
- finally got to bring his passion project,
The Happytime Murders, to theaters.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:01.340
Unfortunately, said passion project was the
opposite of everything his father stood for.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.280
The vulgar puppet-based comedy was far too
raunchy for fans of Henson's work, and executed

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.560
too poorly to engage audiences who showed
up expecting all the raunch.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:14.500
The film constituted a gigantic whiff for
talented star Melissa McCarthy and an inauspicious

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.320
non-Muppet feature debut for Henson.

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:20.440
The film barely made back half its budget
domestically in the wake of withering critiques

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:21.580
and toxic word of mouth.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.300
It's biggest crime, though?

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.640
"It's not funny!

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.460
It's not funny."

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.580
All year long, fans were pumped beyond belief
for The Predator, a fresh new entry in the

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:35.420
series originated by the 1987 Arnold Schwarzenegger
classic Predator.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:39.980
It had a unique spin on the story template,
a great cast including Keegan-Michael Key,

00:03:39.989 --> 00:03:44.200
Thomas Jane and Olivia Munn, and the sure
directorial hand of Shane Black - who, as

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.980
an actor, appeared in the original film.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.880
All the pieces were in place, but a funny
thing happened on the way to the multiplex:

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.900
they forgot to assemble those pieces into
a watchable movie.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.299
Black and company seem to have overestimated
the public appetite for the sequel beyond

00:03:57.299 --> 00:04:01.780
the original's fanbase, and the film itself
simply failed to deliver for those that did

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:02.280
see it.

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:07.700
Aside from being an adaptation of a beloved
novel, A Wrinkle in Time encouraged high expectations

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.519
with a stellar cast and obvious commitment
to diversity.

00:04:10.519 --> 00:04:14.819
It was the first big-budget tentpole film
to be directed by a black woman, Ava DuVernay

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:18.940
, and the cast included Mindy Kaling, Reese
Witherspoon, and Oprah Winfrey.

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:23.360
Unfortunately, audiences simply failed to
materialize, and, perhaps due to lukewarm

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.220
critical response, the usual Young Adult crowd
declined to turn out as well.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:32.110
The film's convoluted marketing certainly
didn't help matters, confusing audiences unfamiliar

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:34.220
with the source material's complicated plot.

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:39.220
Overall, A Wrinkle In Time took in just over
$100 million domestically, and it didn't fare

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:40.720
much better overseas.

00:04:41.420 --> 00:04:43.700
Action Point had an absolutely golden premise.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:48.000
Based on a notoriously dangerous real-life
New Jersey theme park, the film starred Johnny

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.380
Knoxville of Jackass fame as the irresponsible
owner of Action Point.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:55.740
Featuring plenty of actual stunts from its
seasoned performers, the film promised lowbrow

00:04:55.759 --> 00:04:58.460
belly laughs along the lines of the Jackass
film series.

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.520
However, fans found the theme park narrative
to be awkwardly grafted onto what seemed like

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:03.740
another Jackass movie.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:07.759
Worse, even though Knoxville practically killed
himself making the movie, the stunts were

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.880
seen as uninspired and the laughs were few
and far between.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.640
Action Point's poor execution was its doom.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.440
Action Point struggled to make back even a
quarter of its budget, leading to the very

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:21.759
real possibility that we've seen Johnny Knoxville
grievously injure himself for our amusement

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:22.840
for the last time.

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:28.020
Despite not being a sequel, reboot or adaptation,
The Hurricane Heist looked like guaranteed

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.900
dollar signs due to its high concept and directorial
pedigree.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:36.080
Director Rob Cohen gave us the original Fast
and the Furious and Triple X, both high-octane,

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.840
stunt-heavy films which bloomed into surprisingly
profitable franchises.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:44.550
The plot follows a band of thieves who try
to rob the U.S. Mint during a Category 5 storm,

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:48.370
and the film appeared to be a welcome throwback
to the intense, Die Hard-esque thrillers of

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:49.000
yesteryear.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:52.360
Unfortunately, The Hurricane Heist threw back
just a bit too hard.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.000
“Well, this hurricane can change people
from alive to dead.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.060
Se we gotta go.”

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.580
“Yeah, I've seen them before, brother."

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.400
Reviews skewered the movie for being ridiculously
derivative, full of plot-holes, and poorly

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:03.900
shot.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:08.320
Audiences stayed away in droves; the movie
failed to break $1 million on its opening

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.200
day, and finished its theatrical run with
a return of barely $6 million on a budget

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.180
six times that.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.700
Winchester appeared to have both the premise
and the pedigree to be a potential horror

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:19.200
classic.

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:23.600
Dame Helen Mirren starred as Sarah Winchester,
a woman who spent decades building addition

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:25.620
after addition onto her famous mansion.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:29.879
The real Sarah Winchester may have been plagued
by poor mental health and too much time and

00:06:29.879 --> 00:06:33.960
money, but Winchester posited that she was
driven by something more supernatural.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:39.550
Writer/directors the Spierig Brothers delivered
a nifty sci-fi thriller in 2014's Predestination,

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:43.900
but their previous gig had been as guns for
hire on Jigsaw, which was less well-received.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:48.200
With Winchester they displayed a distinct
lack of interest in elevating the material,

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:51.160
instead relying on cheap jump scares and questionable
plotting.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.940
Many critics wondered how they were able to
coax the great Mirren into appearing in the

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:55.440
film.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:00.400
Hotel Artemis is a futuristic sci-fi thriller
boasting a cast that any first-time feature

00:07:00.409 --> 00:07:05.870
director would trade a pinky for: Jodie Foster,
Sterling K. Brown, Charlie Day, Dave Bautista

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:07.620
and Jeff Goldblum, to name a few.

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:12.330
The story revolved around a secret hospital
for criminals in a dystopian future, but director

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:16.509
Drew Pierce's preference for style over substance
resulted in what could have been a mind-blowing

00:07:16.509 --> 00:07:18.250
premise falling curiously flat.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:21.300
Worse, the film's marketing did nothing to
pull audiences in.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:25.879
Despite working from his own screenplay, Pearce
managed to deliver a picture that felt half-baked

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:26.380
and aimless.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.320
Even its kindest critics viewed it as little
more than "pretty cool to look at," which

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:32.800
was nowhere near enough to engage audiences.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:37.520
Despite its modest $15 million budget, Hotel
Artemis didn't even crack half that at the

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:42.460
domestic box office - as any good hotel owner
will tell you, you just can't turn a profit

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:43.940
with that kind of vacancy rate.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:48.880
Annihilation, based on the acclaimed novel
of the same name, is by most accounts a very

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:49.480
good film.

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:53.960
Natalie Portman heads up an amazing cast alongside
Oscar Isaac and Tessa Thompson.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.499
The film's heady premise and assured direction
by director Alex Garland won over critics

00:07:58.499 --> 00:08:01.479
- but audiences responded with surprising
indifference.

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:08.540
"I don't know where it was or what it was."

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:09.800
“How is that possible?”

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:14.520
Movies aimed at adults have struggled in recent
years, and Paramount may have exacerbated

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:19.160
this problem by striking a deal for Annihilation's
international rights with Netflix before release.

00:08:19.480 --> 00:08:23.169
The film dropped on Netflix around the time
of its theatrical release in virtually every

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:28.060
market except North America and China, severely
cutting into its overseas profitability.

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:32.890
The film failed to make back its $40 million
budget, but considering all the rave reviews,

00:08:32.890 --> 00:08:36.300
everyone who took a pass at the theater will
almost certainly catch it eventually - on

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:37.180
Netflix.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:42.220
NBC's This Is Us has become one of network
television's breakout hits of the last few

00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:47.320
years, so it stood to reason that the melodramatic,
family-oriented formula could translate to

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:49.040
success on the big screen as well.

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:53.720
At least, that was Amazon Studios' thinking;
they recruited series creator Dan Fogelman

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:59.130
to write and direct Life Itself, a similarly-themed
feature with an all-star cast including Olivia

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.530
Wilde, Oscar Isaac, and Antonio Banderas.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:06.550
However, Life Itself struggled to a $4.1 million
dollar domestic gross, which can only partially

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:09.410
be chalked up to absolutely brutal reviews.

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:13.350
Critics who caught its debut at the Toronto
International Film Festival ripped it to bits,

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.650
and audiences completely ignored the film.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:19.610
For his part, Fogelman blamed its failure
on critics who didn't understand the film

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:23.900
rather than take responsibility for his almost
comically tragic, over-the-top misfire.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:29.080
Medieval epics are notoriously hit and miss,
and the latest big-screen iteration of Robin

00:09:29.089 --> 00:09:31.480
Hood was far more miss than hit.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:36.570
Critics savaged the film for its forced edginess,
winking anachronisms and emphasis on spectacle,

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:39.320
none of which would have mattered if the film
had hooked audiences.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.340
But they were a little busy the weekend of
its release - flocking to see Ralph Breaks

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:46.820
the Internet and Creed II, two eagerly anticipated
sequels.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:51.900
When the dust cleared, Robin Hood failed to
scrape together even half of its budget domestically.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:56.570
The film's aggressive marketing campaign probably
didn't help matters, as it seems audiences

00:09:56.570 --> 00:10:00.670
simply found no purpose in an overly edgy
take on a tale that Hollywood has been telling

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:02.029
for over a century.

00:10:02.029 --> 00:10:06.790
The $100 million film is one of the biggest
money losers of the last couple years.

00:10:06.790 --> 00:10:09.940
Ironically, it seems like Robin Hood missed
the mark.

